1 NAME Thomas Latham Esq.
1 SEX M
1 BAPM
2 ADDR
3 STAE Greater London
3 CTRY UK
1 BIRT
2 DATE 25 NOV 1744
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE Greater London
3 CTRY UK
1 DEAT
2 DATE SEP 1818
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE Greater London
3 CTRY UK
1 OCCU Groom
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas was baptised on the 25th of November 1744.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father Thomas died in 1750 (aged 31),  he had left the family "in low circumstances with four children which his wife was not able to provide for".
2 CONT
2 CONT Susannah "petitioned" that Thomas be "admitted" to Christ's Hospital School, "to be educated and brought up among other poor children". He was 8 at the time and her only child admitted to the school.  Whilst at the school, his mot
2 CONC her died when he was 10 years old (1754).  She was only 30.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was "discharged" on 15th April 1760 "for use" by his Uncle Thomas Vaughton who was living in Hemmings Row. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This marked the end of the family's misfortunes and illustrates the importance of decent educataion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas later lived in St Dunstan's Hill (1783), Great Tower (1784-1807) and Champion Hill, Camberwell, Surrey (now Southwark/Lambeth) from 1808 until his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is thought to have traded in wines.  The will of Thomas Latham 1744-1818 dated 11/6/1818 mentions William Pulsford and Robert Pulsford. They had a firm of Latham and Pulsford which we believe were wine merchants. Later in 1833 
2 CONC Thomas' son Alfred married Frances Pulsford of Wimpole Street, London and Linslade Manor, Buckinghamshire. Alfred went on to be a governor of the Bank of England. I have a transcript of the will and can e_mail it to you.  They ope
2 CONC rated out 98 Great Tower St and 23 Leadenhall St.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later traded in commodities owning "plantations, estates, messuages, lands, tenements, hereditments in Sion Hill" (Barbados) and Antigua all in the West Indies, importing sugar cane (possibly cotton) into Britain (most likely L
2 CONC iverpool).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is strong evidence that he lived both in London and Liverpool as several of his children married in Liverpool such as Anne who married Daniel Willink and Arthur who married Susannah Roach. It is known that he was a member of
2 CONC  The Glover's Company (1810) and member of Clothworkers Company from 1773. He married twice and is said to have had 22 children of which only 7 reached maturity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will, he left 2000 pounds to "Christ's Hospital (school)" and mentions his executors as being his "friends Robert Pulsford, William Coningham, William Pulsford and George Dobson (the latter two being described as City of Lo
2 CONC ndon Merchants".
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a parishioner of St Giles, Camberwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Executors of his will were his wife and friends Robert Pulsford and William Coningham; properties are recorded in Great Britain, the West Indies and elsewhere; bequest of Â£2000 made to Christâ€™s Hospital, London. (£179682 in tod
2 CONC ay's value http://www.moneysorter.co.uk/calculator_inflation.html 2011).
