1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She attended Winchester College for Girls in England (finishing school) and on her way back (by ship) met Jack Latham who was on his way to Kenya (in the Service of HM Forces) in 1921.
2 CONT
2 CONT After marrying Jack in 1922, she would have accompanied him to Nairobi in Kenya and then to Johannesburg (27 Prince of  Wales Terrace, Parktown first, then Bixley Lodge on Parktown Terrace and Flat no.4, 8 Junction Ave. Parktown).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Jacks death in 1941, Margaret "did not do very well".  The house in Hollowdene had been donated for use to the Red Cross and Margared and her youngest son Michael moved to an appartment "across from the Country Club in Johan
2 CONC nesburg".  Her son's Peter and David were at Hilton College.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After a year or two, she moved to Durban and enrolled her son Michael int Clifton Preparitory School in Lambert Rd. During this time, she resided at the Marine Hotel (Esplanade- I think) where she met and married a British Naval O
2 CONC ffice (described as "a skunk looking for money" by David latham- her son).  The marriage only lasted a few months.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Michael was enrolled at Hilton College, she then moved back to Hollowdene "to contend with brokers/banks for screwing up" her husband's investments.  Finally Hollowdene was sold to Carl Rolfs "one of her boy friends" and M
2 CONC argaret moved to Jan Smuts Avenue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Peter was "in the Service" during this time until he was wounded in Italy and sent home. Son David went to study in Canada (Vancouver).
2 CONT
2 CONT David provided most of the above information.  He further wrote of his mother "I saw her with my wife Nancy in England around 1954 then a little later when she visited us in New Jersey in around 1956.  That was the last time I saw
2 CONC  her- she died in 1963.  She was moving around quite a bit and didn't have a very happy life after Dad was killed".
