1 OCCU Farmer and Grazier - family farm and later own small property.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kenneth Bertram Latham was born on 1st April, 1918 in Mosman, NSW, Australia and was the third child to Leslie Frederick Latham and Glads Caroline Latham. He grew up on the family farm "Burragurrum", Baan Baa, NSW 
2 CONC along with his parents and three brothers (Desmond, Phillips, and Guy) . Kenneth was nicknamed "Bunty" as a child and it stayed for much of his life. He was educated on the farm with the tutelage of his parents, and significantly 
2 CONC his father, via Blackfriars Correspondence School, Camperdown, NSW, Australia. As a child his principal interests were art and drawing, sport, nature and farm life. At the earliest possible age he ceased schooling and commenced wo
2 CONC rking on the family farm which was a wheat and sheep property. He was an excellent horse man and brook-in and trained numerous horses, he was very good at cricket and excellent in tennis - becoming the North West NSW Champion Tenn
2 CONC is player. Kenneth (Bunty) joined the Boggabri Light Horse in 1937 along with two of his brothers; Phillips and Guy. They formed a team which included Victor Kennsit, a neighbour and won the "Section of Four over hurdles" for coor
2 CONC dinated jumping in the light horse. In 1939, for his 21st Birthday he elected to spend money given by his Grandfather Frederick on a cruise up and down the east coast of Australia on the "Monunda". It was on this cruise he meet hi
2 CONC s future wife, Margaret Stewart MacDougal who was returning from Brisbane to Sydney. Kenneth studied Bookkeeping and did private agricultural studies with the assistance of the local agronomist. With the outbreak of the Second Wor
2 CONC ld War, his three brothers joined the Australian Airforce, but on doctors orders, Kenneth was made to work the family farm and was part of the Narribri Defence Force (VDF). He married Margaret on the 25th October, 1941 at Scotts K
2 CONC Irk, Mosman. Their reception was at the Royal Kirribilli Yacht Squadron and the honeymoon at Austinmer Beach on the South Coast of NSW. Ken and Margaret had several still born children while working the farm during the war. Ken ev
2 CONC entually agreed to adopt a daughter in 1950 and Deborah Anne Latham was born 5th October, 1950. They took a 'suitable' offer for a second child 18 months later and the second adopted child David Kenneth Latham was born 5th Februar
2 CONC y, 1952. Due to family conflict Kenneth and Margaret left the family farm in 1956 and Ken commenced managing properties around NSW for the next 10 years. In 1960, Ken became the Manager of 'Baramul Stud' in the Widden Valley, NSW 
2 CONC a 8,000 acre Thoroughbred and Angus stud for several years. The stud stood Star Kingdom, the Irish born sprinter who was named champion Australian sire five times, champion brood mare sire three times and sired 65 Stakes winners a
2 CONC nd 260 individual winners and the progeny dominated the early years of the Golden Slipper. He also managed 'Kerrabee Park' in the Kerrabee (Upper Hunter) Valley, NSW and two other properties in this time. In 1966 the family moved 
2 CONC to Mosman to allow Margaret to nurse her Aunts in their final years. Ken was employed as Senior Constable / Ranger at Bradleys Head, Mosman. He loved the couple of years spent on harbour at Bradley's Head and did some excellent st
2 CONC one mason and sandstone boarder work there that remains today. Following the passing of Margaret's Aunt's the family purchased 180 acres at Leura, NSW - the hobby farm was "Fairmarch" and a small number of horse and cattle were ru
2 CONC n. They loved the 12 years spent on this farm and entertained generously. Following the sale of "Fairmarch", they lived in Katoomba for a couple of years before moving to "Biwa", 255 Bentinck Street, Bathurst to retire and live th
2 CONC e remainder of their lives. Ken was a tall, handsome man with excellent sense of humour, always young at heart and remained independent, totally cognitive with great whit and relatively fit till he died 3 months short of his 90 bi
2 CONC rthday.   
