1 NAME Thomas /Lathom/ of Scarisbrick
2 GIVN Thomas
2 NSFX of Scarisbrick
2 _MARNM Lathom
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1675
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 23 OCT 1716
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Refered to as "Sir" on http://webspace.webring.com/people/ls/stephhunt/ps02/ps02_049.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Thought to be Tho. Lathom - Burial: 23 Oct 1716 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England Abode: Scaris[brick] Register: Burial 1699 - 1743, Page 143, Entry 99 Source: LDS Film 1849663 see http://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Ormsk
2 CONC irk/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT He is either the above Thomas or the following, Thomas Lathom - Burial: 26 Feb 1723/4 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England Abode: Scarisbrick Register: Burial 1699 - 1743, Page 154, Entry 116 Source: LDS Film 184966
2 CONC 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Both married an Elizabeth.  She is either Elizabeth. Lathom - Wife of Thomas Lathom Burial: 22 Mar 1708/9 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England Abode: Scaris[brick]
2 CONT    or Elizabeth Latham wife of Thomas Latham Burial: 14 Oct 1719 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England Abode: Scarisbrick  Register: Burial 1699 - 1743, Page 149, Entry 25 Source: LDS Film 1849663 also http://www.lan
2 CONC -opc.org.uk/Ormskirk/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "That many people now living in South-West Lancashire or elsewhere are descended from the Lathoms of Parbold is certain, although of few indeed could any evidence now he brought of their ancestry; but this enquiry into Peter Latho
2 CONC m’s life history has elicited the possibility that the late Mr. James Bromley’s belief that his wife was also descended from the Lathoms of Parbold, was correct, and, if so it must have been through a certain Thomas Lathom of Scar
2 CONC isbrick (25}, who was a contemporary of Peter Lathom the founder of the Charity. Assuming for the moment that both men descend from the Lathoms of Parbold, though it must again be emphasised that there is no actual proof  of the r
2 CONC elationship, then both men were grandsons of the children of Richard Lathom and Elizabeth Leigh (17). Between Lathom of Scarisbrick, the undoubted ancestor of Mrs James Bromley and myself, and any of the Lathoms of Parbold there i
2 CONC s a missing generation, of whom I have no evidence. Mr Russell has made the intriguing suggestion that the gap may be filled in the manner indicated in the pedigree, following the custom of naming the first son after the grandfath
2 CONC er and the second son after the father. In the pedigree these hypothetical ancestors are in italics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the date of birth of Thomas Lathom of Scarisbrick (25) there is unfortunately no evidence, just as there is none of that of William Lathom of Haigh (22), the assumed father of Peter Lathom. We know they existed, but know nothin
2 CONC g more. But the similarities of time, place, general conditions, and the astonishing repetition of the Parbold christian names –Edward, Thomas, John and Richard - coupled with the repetition of  “the grandfather’s name. then the f
2 CONC ather’s “ in Peter Lathom’s case as also in Mrs Bromley’s, with a similar absence of evidence of birth at. Precisely the same time and place in the genealogical tree would appear to be too unusual to be lightly dismissed as just a
2 CONC  mere coincidence."
2 CONT
2 CONT Be this as it may the fact remains that the only break in the long line of descent from the Founder of Burscough Priory through the younger branches of the Lathom family to the present time (1946) occurs between the children of Ri
2 CONC chard and Elizabeth Lathom (17) and Thomas Lathom of Scarisbrick (25). This single break apart, the genealogical tree is complete, thoroughly established and well documented. In its later phases it comes about in this way. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1670, any Lathom's living on the Allerton Estate would have been forced to vacated to nearby towns.  It is well known that Thomas resided in Scarisbrick. It is not known what he would have done for a living, but it is almost
2 CONC  certain that the family was poor along with other Royalist family's at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Lathom of Scarisbrick {25} had four sons - Edward, Thomas, John and Richard, -once more the grandfather’s name, then the father's-  baptized at Ormskirk Parish Church in the years 1692, 1694, 1697 and 1699 respectively. 
