3 CITY Ormskirk
3 CTRY UK
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Richard Lathom, born in 1699, and the youngest. Son of Thomas Lathom of Scarisbrick (25). He had at least eight children,  Elizabeth, Richard, Sara, Rachael, Anne, Alice and Martha, Their dates of birth range from 
2 CONC 1726 to 1741. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sara Lathom (29) was Christened at Ormskirk Parish Church on the 8th April 1729. At the age of 42 she married at the same church on the 7th April.. 1771, Richard  Berry, of whom she was the second wife. like most of the women of h
2 CONC er time she could not write so made her mark in the register. Her husband, a son of Peter Berry, of Lathom, was christened at Ormskirk Church on 28th May 1728. He died in 1819 at the age of 91, and is buried in Ormskirk churchyard
2 CONC , where his tombstone now forms part of the side walk leading to the Derby chapel. Both his fathaer and grandfather were called Peter, the former born about 1700 whilst the latter was christened at Ormskirk on the 1st April. 1660.
2 CONC  Of the Berrys or Buries there are, in the Ormskirk Parish registers, even earlier records, though whether these refer to the same family as the Peter Berry just mentioned is by no means certain. A Peter Berry or Burie was christe
2 CONC ned at Ormskirk on the 16th May  1574, and yet another on the 31st July 1580.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Berry and Sara Lathom had a son. Peter, (31) born in 1772, who died at the age of 81, he too is buried at Ormskirk. He married Jane Pilkington by whom be had a son Richard, (39) Mrs. Bromley’s uncle, and my grandfather, wh
2 CONC o was born in 1806 and died at Burscough in .June 1906 at the great age of 100 years and four months. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachael Lathom, (29) Sara's younger sister, was christened at Ormskirk Parish Church on the 16th July, 1731. She later married, also at Orrnskirk, a man called Heyes. To him she bore a son, Richard. whose second child, Margaret (3
2 CONC 9) married my maternal grandfather, John Barlow, Head Master of Upholland Grammar School. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The three Berrys just mentioned. Richard, Peter, and Richard, afford a remarkable instance of longevity, as they cover a period  of 178 years. from 1728 to 1906. At the time of the battle of  Waterloo, Richard Berry the centenaria
2 CONC n, was a boy of 9 years. and he has often related how well he remembered being taken to Ormskirk Church on Sunday morning to hear the village blacksmith or other worthy, perched on a tombstone, telling of the the latest news about
2 CONC  Napoleon Bonaparte. He in his turn,could recall some of his grandfather’s recollections of thc local doings attaching to the invasion of England by the young Pretender in 1745. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See also http://webspace.webring.com/people/ls/stephhunt/ps02/ps02_052.htm
